1
00:00:03,868 --> 00:00:09,610
[SPEAKER_00]: There are suttas where wanderers of other sects also teach the five hindrances.

2
00:00:11,830 --> 00:00:31,116
[SPEAKER_00]: But when asked about the difference between their teaching and the Buddha's teaching, the Buddha replies that if those wanderers of other sects were asked if there is a way of exposition by which the five hindrances become ten, then they would not know how to answer and become all confused.

3
00:00:32,606 --> 00:00:36,609
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha then goes on to teach how the five hindrances become ten.

4
00:00:37,329 --> 00:00:47,176
[SPEAKER_00]: And he says, whatever sensual desire there is for the internal is a hindrance, and whatever sensual desire there is for the external is also a hindrance.

5
00:00:47,997 --> 00:00:49,017
[SPEAKER_00]: Same for ill-will.

6
00:00:50,118 --> 00:00:53,540
[SPEAKER_00]: Can we discuss what is meant by internal and external here?

7
00:00:58,856 --> 00:01:17,169
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I think the most practical, basically, way for somebody to wrap their head around it, would be external, is regarding the sense objects.

8
00:01:20,091 --> 00:01:25,875
[SPEAKER_01]: But that also means your thoughts concerned about sense objects.

9
00:01:28,415 --> 00:01:29,216
[SPEAKER_00]: would be external.

10
00:01:33,559 --> 00:01:41,845
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, internal, again, practically speaking, say, you could discern the internal.

11
00:01:41,945 --> 00:01:50,251
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just turn your senses or your attention towards it, because that's not... it never appears as an object of your senses.

12
00:01:50,291 --> 00:01:55,535
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you know, you could say, well, internally, it's the sense basis and so on, but it's like, what is sense basis, practically speaking?

13
00:01:56,913 --> 00:02:00,406
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you... how do you look for them when you can't see them directly?

14
00:02:00,426 --> 00:02:02,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Your eye cannot see itself.

15
00:02:04,136 --> 00:02:05,217
[SPEAKER_01]: You can only see the sights.

16
00:02:05,577 --> 00:02:11,924
[SPEAKER_01]: And if, for whatever reason, your eye sees the organ of itself in the mirror, it sees the sight of it.

17
00:02:11,944 --> 00:02:13,485
[SPEAKER_01]: It does not see itself seeing it.

18
00:02:14,446 --> 00:02:27,619
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way to discern the internal, it's that kind of, as you often say, indirect way through that, like a peripheral recognition or, like, even, like, self-awareness.

19
00:02:28,304 --> 00:02:36,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, awareness of yourself in a situation, that's already a form of experiencing the internal counterpart.

20
00:02:38,694 --> 00:02:45,401
[SPEAKER_01]: So, instead of you just being absorbed with the senses, you see yourself or discern yourself being absorbed with the sense objects.

21
00:02:45,461 --> 00:02:46,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry, not senses.

22
00:02:47,242 --> 00:02:53,048
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you being... instead of being absorbed with the sense objects, you see yourself being absorbed with the sense objects.

23
00:02:54,494 --> 00:02:58,375
[SPEAKER_01]: That second experience is like revealing the internal counterpart.

24
00:02:59,056 --> 00:03:02,997
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, you could have... Well, and sensuality obviously permeates both.

25
00:03:04,077 --> 00:03:07,058
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, sensuality is manifested through chasing the sense objects.

26
00:03:07,979 --> 00:03:11,400
[SPEAKER_01]: But, fundamentally, both perspectives

27
00:03:12,544 --> 00:03:20,969
[SPEAKER_01]: That can be simultaneously present, your kind of focus on a sense object, but then recognizing yourself being absorbed with a sense object.

28
00:03:21,469 --> 00:03:30,494
[SPEAKER_01]: Both of those perspectives can be concurrent, and they are concurrent, it's just that you don't necessarily discern them both, but they're both within the domain of centrality.

29
00:03:32,075 --> 00:03:36,538
[SPEAKER_01]: So they would be both affected with ill-will, or with the possibility of ill-will.

30
00:03:37,941 --> 00:04:00,547
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, it's not enough to just say no to sense objects, for example, that you are essentially sort of craving for, but you need to sufficiently also see yourself in the situation of craving for sense objects, and then lose, overcome the passion towards that situation of the reflexive kind.

31
00:04:04,708 --> 00:04:11,730
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what the Buddha was describing in the suttas, like, yeah, you can be physically... you need to be physically withdrawn from the sensuality.

32
00:04:12,691 --> 00:04:17,552
[SPEAKER_01]: But if your mind is also not dried up from it, you're not overcoming it.

33
00:04:19,273 --> 00:04:21,173
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that's what means drying up your mind.

34
00:04:21,273 --> 00:04:29,256
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, you can, as I said, say you see something pleasant, something desirable, and desire is present.

35
00:04:31,393 --> 00:04:39,740
[SPEAKER_01]: If you just obviously act out of it and pursue it, there is no... there is no concurrent recognition of that other internal perspective.

36
00:04:40,461 --> 00:04:50,449
[SPEAKER_01]: There is just continuation of the loss of perspective and going down the rabbit hole until kind of it runs out, options disappear, evaporate, and then you might kind of wake up afterwards.

37
00:04:51,210 --> 00:04:56,915
[SPEAKER_01]: You scratch your itch so hard and then it stops itching and then it's like, oh, where am I?

38
00:04:57,055 --> 00:04:57,556
[SPEAKER_01]: What happened?

39
00:04:57,576 --> 00:04:58,036
[SPEAKER_01]: Type of thing.

40
00:04:58,056 --> 00:04:59,017
[SPEAKER_01]: You kind of come out of a

41
00:04:59,774 --> 00:05:05,616
[SPEAKER_01]: That haze of being kind of pulled further into centrality, because you gave in.

42
00:05:06,517 --> 00:05:25,785
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're not, say, automatically doing that, but are still staying on the level of, okay, this is the beautiful sight that is trying to pressure me, I'll say no to it, I'll say no to it, I'll say no to it, or let me think something else, let me look at something else, let me let me look at the ugly to remove it, let me do this, let me do that,

43
00:05:26,463 --> 00:05:31,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, you're still not self-aware of the experience as a whole.

44
00:05:32,285 --> 00:05:34,426
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the internal dimension is still not revealed.

45
00:05:35,206 --> 00:05:36,107
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still not discerned.

46
00:05:36,127 --> 00:05:49,091
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why I keep saying to people, the practice of a subha that the suttas describe, or practice of metta, applies to somebody, a sotāpanna, basically, who already sees the internal, sufficiently to the point he has discerned citta.

47
00:05:50,512 --> 00:05:53,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Because citta can only be discerned further internally, so to speak.

48
00:05:55,434 --> 00:05:59,657
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, if you just think when you see beautiful, look at the ugly.

49
00:06:00,477 --> 00:06:02,158
[SPEAKER_01]: When you think bad, think positive.

50
00:06:03,099 --> 00:06:08,622
[SPEAKER_01]: You see, either way, you remain engaged with the sense objects and its oppositions.

51
00:06:09,283 --> 00:06:12,865
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't see yourself being engaged with the sense objects.

52
00:06:12,985 --> 00:06:19,609
[SPEAKER_01]: That is not permitted to arise, because obviously your view is still locked onto sense object as the problem.

53
00:06:20,550 --> 00:06:22,231
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not where the sensuality comes from.

54
00:06:24,365 --> 00:06:31,208
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could be countering sense objects with your ugly sense objects, but even that's not the actual practice of a subha.

55
00:06:32,929 --> 00:06:39,171
[SPEAKER_01]: A subha is when the... there is a sense object that has... that's pressuring the mind with the desire.

56
00:06:39,972 --> 00:06:42,053
[SPEAKER_01]: You recognize the experience as a whole.

57
00:06:42,333 --> 00:06:47,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, there is... he... as the satipaṭṭhāna, he knows the mind of lust as mind of lust.

58
00:06:48,179 --> 00:06:52,260
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he knows there is a mind infected with lust towards this.

59
00:06:52,681 --> 00:06:54,661
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what I see, the whole thing.

60
00:06:55,502 --> 00:07:01,183
[SPEAKER_01]: And now, while the whole thing is there, I can also provide the mind on the basis of the whole thing.

61
00:07:01,964 --> 00:07:14,108
[SPEAKER_01]: I can provide it with the... well, that very thing that you are infatuated with, body or other tastes or sounds and so on, is dangerous.

62
00:07:15,311 --> 00:07:16,872
[SPEAKER_01]: Care is discussed in and of itself.

63
00:07:17,572 --> 00:07:21,953
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now you are rewriting the context of the involvement of desire.

64
00:07:21,993 --> 00:07:23,914
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not just trying to deny it and replace it.

65
00:07:25,094 --> 00:07:29,876
[SPEAKER_01]: And that can only be done, that rewriting, can only be done internally, on the basis of internal.

66
00:07:30,436 --> 00:07:34,557
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you are just dealing with the external stuff, you're not overcoming a hindrance.

67
00:07:36,458 --> 00:07:43,260
[SPEAKER_01]: But if through obvious necessary restraint towards the external, you do not just blindly try to deny

68
00:07:44,184 --> 00:07:55,188
[SPEAKER_01]: When the mind is affected with lust, or ill will, or doubt, but you recognize, instead of that, you recognize the experience as a whole, of mind infected towards this, mind affected towards that.

69
00:07:57,649 --> 00:08:03,751
[SPEAKER_01]: And you cultivate that recognition, instead of just giving in, either through just acting out, or trying to deny it.

70
00:08:05,072 --> 00:08:11,194
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you develop internal sense bases sufficiently enough, so when then both are included.

71
00:08:12,352 --> 00:08:27,788
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when basically, when all you can see is your mind having a desire towards this sight or that sound, or any object, when that's your starting point, means you would have overcome the view of sensuality, the assumption of sensuality.

72
00:08:28,585 --> 00:08:37,829
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you just see the mind wanting or not wanting, but you're completely disowning the whole thing, because it has been discerned as, oh, I'm not doing this.

73
00:08:37,849 --> 00:08:38,750
[SPEAKER_01]: I were never doing this.

74
00:08:39,030 --> 00:08:41,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Even these desires that I thought were mine, were never mine.

75
00:08:42,592 --> 00:08:52,776
[SPEAKER_01]: I just did not see the internal framework, because I've been preoccupied of just acting towards sense objects out of the immediate pressure, either through affirming it or denying it.

76
00:08:52,796 --> 00:08:55,778
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, I was not looking internally.

77
00:08:56,598 --> 00:09:11,550
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why the beginning of that internal recognition is self-awareness, like, in any situation, in any experience, pleasant, unpleasant or neutral.

78
00:09:13,332 --> 00:09:17,235
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why we say, like, in the beginning, you have to endure things rightly.

79
00:09:18,664 --> 00:09:23,105
[SPEAKER_01]: To get to see that, hold on, it's not about getting rid of stuff in the way I thought it was.

80
00:09:23,425 --> 00:09:24,946
[SPEAKER_01]: Just counter it, just do the opposite.

81
00:09:25,426 --> 00:09:29,867
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're still doing that equally lacking awareness of that internal counterpart.

82
00:09:30,348 --> 00:09:31,848
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's about including everything.

83
00:09:32,548 --> 00:09:42,351
[SPEAKER_01]: So, first, if you habitually stop giving up, giving towards it, like immediately jumping at the... what things... whatever your pressure towards, if you just immediately go for it, well, that's a problem.

84
00:09:43,012 --> 00:09:44,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever your pressure towards,

85
00:09:44,871 --> 00:09:49,174
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't immediately go for it, but you try to now get rid of that experience as a whole.

86
00:09:49,594 --> 00:09:54,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, you're trying to get rid of that... the basis where the internal can be discerned.

87
00:09:56,098 --> 00:10:06,224
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you don't give in blindly, but you don't try to get rid of what has arisen, but instead recognize it as the wholeness of your situation, because that is a fact, like, oh, there's a desire towards this.

88
00:10:06,384 --> 00:10:09,926
[SPEAKER_01]: I said no, because I learned, I heard it, I need to restrain myself first.

89
00:10:11,287 --> 00:10:11,727
[SPEAKER_01]: Now what?

90
00:10:12,394 --> 00:10:19,856
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, do I now believe that just through saying no, no, no, mind will magically tame itself and free itself from sensuality?

91
00:10:20,096 --> 00:10:20,236
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

92
00:10:21,137 --> 00:10:21,657
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what do I do?

93
00:10:22,057 --> 00:10:38,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I recognize the experience as a whole of me being pressured by desire towards this or that, saying no to it, feeling uncomfortable on account of it, and now resisting that discomfort with aversion.

94
00:10:40,403 --> 00:10:43,986
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what you would see, if you don't blindly give in and don't try to get rid of it.

95
00:10:44,446 --> 00:10:54,475
[SPEAKER_01]: You will recognize, okay, whether I like it or not, I am in the situation of such-and-such kind, which is the situation I just described.

96
00:10:54,615 --> 00:10:56,937
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm trying to overcome this discomfort.

97
00:10:57,597 --> 00:11:01,000
[SPEAKER_01]: I used to try and overcome it by giving in to sense desires.

98
00:11:01,621 --> 00:11:02,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, I was told that's bad.

99
00:11:02,742 --> 00:11:05,924
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, I was trying to overcome it by doing this, by doing this, by doing that.

100
00:11:06,465 --> 00:11:07,966
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, I'm averse to it.

101
00:11:09,232 --> 00:11:10,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I averse to it?

102
00:11:11,913 --> 00:11:12,694
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not doing it.

103
00:11:13,494 --> 00:11:18,036
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind is gripped, but why do I take for granted the aversion that it provides for me?

104
00:11:20,918 --> 00:11:25,660
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's how you actually overcome hindrances internally and externally.

105
00:11:26,921 --> 00:11:35,025
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you are just seeing them or assuming them to be on the level of external sense objects, you're not overcoming anything.

106
00:11:35,065 --> 00:11:36,666
[SPEAKER_01]: Those are not even hindrances correctly.

107
00:11:46,208 --> 00:12:03,817
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through practice of self-awareness, whether you're trying to go with your desire, or say no, or try to get rid of it because it's unpleasant, first and foremost, you need to be self-aware of whatever the experience is.

108
00:12:03,857 --> 00:12:10,661
[SPEAKER_01]: And then factually recognize, okay, yeah, mind, citta, is still liable towards these pressures, towards these pulls.

109
00:12:11,751 --> 00:12:15,332
[SPEAKER_01]: But I can't just force it, to drop it.

110
00:12:15,372 --> 00:12:16,793
[SPEAKER_01]: Force the mind to just give it up.

111
00:12:17,593 --> 00:12:21,555
[SPEAKER_01]: But what I can do is never lose perspective in regard to that whole thing.

112
00:12:21,735 --> 00:12:26,276
[SPEAKER_01]: So again, satipaṭṭhāna says he knows the mind of lust as the mind of lust.

113
00:12:26,316 --> 00:12:30,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Does not say, when mind of lust, he does this and he makes it into a mind of non-lust.

114
00:12:30,938 --> 00:12:31,078
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

115
00:12:32,207 --> 00:12:35,808
[SPEAKER_01]: You will make it into a mind of non-lust by knowing it as a lustful mind.

116
00:12:36,468 --> 00:12:38,989
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you will subdue it and calm it down.

117
00:12:39,609 --> 00:12:45,230
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you try to act out of the mind of lust, any act you do will be infected with lust.

118
00:12:45,870 --> 00:12:51,712
[SPEAKER_01]: If you try to act out of mind of ill-will, anything you do, even practice of metta, will be driven by that ill-will.

119
00:12:52,992 --> 00:12:59,354
[SPEAKER_01]: So he knows the mind of lust as mind of lust, self-aware to the extent of recognizing the citta for what it is,

120
00:13:00,360 --> 00:13:02,981
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, not losing the sight of that until it calms down.

121
00:13:03,801 --> 00:13:07,083
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's where the patient and patient endurance, khanti, comes in.

122
00:13:09,763 --> 00:13:11,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, you'll be just doing it on your terms.

123
00:13:11,564 --> 00:13:14,265
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, otherwise, you'll be just doing it out of your own desire.

124
00:13:16,126 --> 00:13:18,147
[SPEAKER_00]: And the same applies to the hindrance of doubt.

125
00:13:18,407 --> 00:13:18,707
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

126
00:13:19,187 --> 00:13:22,748
[SPEAKER_01]: I think doubt is probably going to be the first one that's kind of the most obvious.

127
00:13:23,669 --> 00:13:24,609
[SPEAKER_01]: If you can, eh...

128
00:13:28,583 --> 00:13:42,410
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if you can practice correctly, towards the hindrance of doubt internally and externally, you will overcome the view, the fetter of doubt.

129
00:13:43,290 --> 00:13:55,136
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you still might have doubt manifest internally or externally, but you would not be established upon a view that the doubt comes from the external counterparts.

130
00:13:57,746 --> 00:13:59,867
[SPEAKER_01]: And a person might say, well, I don't think that.

131
00:13:59,967 --> 00:14:07,310
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact is, just ask yourself, when there is a doubtful mind, do you immediately go and start revolving around fixing it?

132
00:14:08,090 --> 00:14:10,371
[SPEAKER_01]: Resolving the doubt, finding the answers.

133
00:14:11,471 --> 00:14:14,393
[SPEAKER_01]: That means you still carry the view that the answers come from the outside.

134
00:14:15,813 --> 00:14:22,316
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's like people can't even follow their own reasoning, their own intellect, even when it's accurate.

135
00:14:23,190 --> 00:14:31,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it doesn't carry as much weight for them, that knowledge, as opposed to knowledge of some authority externally saying what's right, what's wrong.

136
00:14:32,958 --> 00:14:45,629
[SPEAKER_01]: Or sometimes people can't even, like, people know what they should do internally, like, through their own intellect and reasoning and understanding, but they can't do it until somebody externally says, even if it's the same thing that somebody externally tells them, that's the way where they can do it.

137
00:14:46,643 --> 00:15:03,866
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the view has placed the emphasis that the resolution of this doubt, of this pressure, discomfort, dissonance, that doesn't feel right, comes through finding the external clarity and external means of affirmation of what I need to do, what I need to practice, and so on.

138
00:15:04,666 --> 00:15:09,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Initially, everybody will start like that, but the point will come where you will know that...

139
00:15:09,667 --> 00:15:12,268
[SPEAKER_01]: I know enough, in a sense, of information and learning,

140
00:15:13,495 --> 00:15:19,977
[SPEAKER_01]: But what I'm doing now is, like, whenever the mind pressures me with doubt, I take it for granted that I must get rid of this.

141
00:15:20,077 --> 00:15:20,997
[SPEAKER_01]: I must resolve this.

142
00:15:21,417 --> 00:15:24,698
[SPEAKER_01]: So I go back and I reread the text and I ask more questions and I do this.

143
00:15:25,218 --> 00:15:27,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Or I change up and I find something else.

144
00:15:27,939 --> 00:15:37,282
[SPEAKER_01]: Something to just appease this mind externally, which is no different than finding sense objects, pursuing sense objects to appease the mental craving, the citta's craving towards sense desire.

145
00:15:38,062 --> 00:15:38,762
[SPEAKER_01]: Through sense desire.

146
00:15:40,143 --> 00:15:54,240
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why, like, if you actually don't give in to the pressure of doubt, and stay with it, and instead of, but I must resolve this because I'm not sure about it, instead of no, I will resolve it when there is no more pressure to resolve it.

147
00:15:55,061 --> 00:15:59,347
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, I might choose to resolve it, if there is a genuine lack of information I'm not sure about.

148
00:16:00,306 --> 00:16:06,188
[SPEAKER_01]: But for as long as there is this compulsion to resolve it, that it's unpleasant, I will be fueling the hindrance of doubt.

149
00:16:06,989 --> 00:16:11,591
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, I will be maintaining the fetter of the doubt that I... that I'm fettered by.

150
00:16:11,751 --> 00:16:17,833
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, placing the emphasis that doubt comes from the lack of clarity from external things.

151
00:16:19,734 --> 00:16:26,997
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you don't act out of doubt long enough, endure the mental pressure and be self-aware of it,

152
00:16:27,960 --> 00:16:32,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Instead of trying to resolve it, the emphasis will lessen and eventually it would shift.

153
00:16:33,382 --> 00:16:40,744
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will realize that the doubt comes from doubtful citta, that has encountered things suitable for doubt, as the suttas would say.

154
00:16:41,284 --> 00:16:43,245
[SPEAKER_01]: But the doubt never came from the actual objects.

155
00:16:43,843 --> 00:16:45,483
[SPEAKER_01]: that you've been perceiving or thinking about.

156
00:16:45,983 --> 00:16:46,824
[SPEAKER_01]: Same with sensuality.

157
00:16:47,424 --> 00:16:50,644
[SPEAKER_01]: In so many suttas, the Buddha said, no, beautiful things remain in the world.

158
00:16:50,744 --> 00:16:51,705
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not sensuality.

159
00:16:52,345 --> 00:16:57,966
[SPEAKER_01]: It was the man's internal desire and lust, basically, his thoughts affected with that wrong emphasis.

160
00:16:58,086 --> 00:16:59,766
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what his sensuality is.

161
00:17:00,706 --> 00:17:05,007
[SPEAKER_01]: The wise one who removes that desire and lust, beautiful things remain standing unchanged.

162
00:17:05,287 --> 00:17:07,348
[SPEAKER_01]: They never were the origin of the problem.

163
00:17:08,308 --> 00:17:09,528
[SPEAKER_01]: He was the untrained citta.

164
00:17:12,908 --> 00:17:20,141
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is why the sotāpanna is described as both being confident and at the same time independent of others.

165
00:17:20,468 --> 00:17:21,209
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, exactly.

166
00:17:21,629 --> 00:17:38,824
[SPEAKER_01]: He has seen citta sufficiently enough, on that internal basis, that he cannot fall back into that wrong emphasis that the clarity comes from the clarity of the form, or duty, or virtue, or performing the precepts, or external authority.

167
00:17:39,385 --> 00:17:41,026
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just cut off.

168
00:17:41,046 --> 00:17:42,167
[SPEAKER_01]: That whole belief is just cut off.

169
00:17:42,207 --> 00:17:42,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Gone.

170
00:17:42,648 --> 00:17:43,308
[SPEAKER_01]: Cannot come back.

171
00:17:43,989 --> 00:17:58,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the sole reason for the existence of that entire sphere, of those first three fetters, was not seeing the citta as the sole reason for all these problems, for the craving for the dukkha and so on.

172
00:18:01,117 --> 00:18:09,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, once he sees it, he won't be mistaking it elsewhere, but okay, now he needs to train that, and that's the whole training described in the suttas.

173
00:18:09,807 --> 00:18:17,655
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why almost always, when the Buddha outlined practice of a... not almost always, every single time, when the Buddha outlined the practice of a subha or metta,

174
00:18:18,916 --> 00:18:29,020
[SPEAKER_01]: Different perceptions, even that one kind of contemplating the asubha or jhāna, it was always in reference to a noble disciple that already knows what the path is, already been doing it.

175
00:18:29,540 --> 00:18:34,221
[SPEAKER_01]: Because now, when he does the asubha, when he does the metta, he's doing it aiming at the citta.

176
00:18:34,341 --> 00:18:35,202
[SPEAKER_01]: He's doing it rightly.

177
00:18:35,842 --> 00:18:44,785
[SPEAKER_01]: He's not doing it just as a reaction to what's being... what he feels oppresses him on account of saying no to blindly following the desire and acting out.

178
00:18:48,134 --> 00:18:48,975
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the Middle Way.

179
00:18:48,995 --> 00:18:52,178
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the Middle Way sounds simple, yet it's the hardest thing to see.

180
00:18:54,761 --> 00:19:01,909
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if the Buddha... if the Buddha's teaching was... would have been the way the modern Theravada is presenting, it just countered the perceptions.

181
00:19:01,989 --> 00:19:02,850
[SPEAKER_01]: Anybody can do it.

182
00:19:03,110 --> 00:19:06,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter whether you're keeping the precepts or not.

183
00:19:06,153 --> 00:19:07,275
[SPEAKER_01]: When this, just think that.

184
00:19:07,335 --> 00:19:08,576
[SPEAKER_01]: When that, just think this.

185
00:19:08,696 --> 00:19:10,338
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, well, the Buddha would have said so.

186
00:19:11,530 --> 00:19:23,577
[SPEAKER_01]: He would not have been talking about the citta, and the trainee, and the signs of the mind, and the necessary factors for development, and the virtue and restraint, seclusion, withdrawal, for these things to even become possible to be seen.

187
00:19:23,717 --> 00:19:25,958
[SPEAKER_01]: He would have just said, no, monks, when it's this, just look at that.

188
00:19:27,599 --> 00:19:28,820
[SPEAKER_01]: When it's that, just look at this.

189
00:19:30,261 --> 00:19:32,022
[SPEAKER_01]: When this bothers you, recite this mantra.

190
00:19:32,062 --> 00:19:33,743
[SPEAKER_01]: When that bothers you, recite that mantra.

191
00:19:35,744 --> 00:19:40,887
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's just perversion and bastardization, pretty much, by people that haven't seen

192
00:19:42,178 --> 00:20:00,055
[SPEAKER_01]: That middle ground, and all they see is just what's written there, and it's kind of flattened, and it just sounds like, okay, so, when it's A, I do the B, when it's B, if B bothers me, I go back to A, and I just do that long enough until, poof, the mind just enlightens itself.

193
00:20:03,358 --> 00:20:05,420
[SPEAKER_01]: But, like, how can you do jhāna?

194
00:20:06,288 --> 00:20:07,830
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not fully withdrawn from sensuality.

195
00:20:07,850 --> 00:20:13,018
[SPEAKER_01]: Because every single description in the Sutta starts by being fully withdrawn from sensuality as a prerequisite for jhāna.

196
00:20:13,539 --> 00:20:22,652
[SPEAKER_01]: The pleasure of jhāna comes from, not because of a mystical experience of meditation, but comes from being safe from the dangerous domain of sensuality and recognizing that.

197
00:20:25,483 --> 00:20:30,704
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you do metta if you don't see your own citta being sufficiently enough seeing it?

198
00:20:30,764 --> 00:20:32,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just like, oh, yeah, my citta is now like this.

199
00:20:32,604 --> 00:20:32,684
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

200
00:20:32,724 --> 00:20:36,405
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you seeing it as an independent entity inseparable from you?

201
00:20:38,005 --> 00:20:41,606
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you seeing mind affected with ill-will as mind affected with ill-will?

202
00:20:42,106 --> 00:20:44,087
[SPEAKER_01]: And are you... are you okay with that?

203
00:20:45,247 --> 00:20:48,387
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, are you able to contain that mind affected with ill-will?

204
00:20:48,767 --> 00:20:50,568
[SPEAKER_01]: Or are you, okay, but now let me do metta.

205
00:20:51,348 --> 00:20:51,568
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

206
00:20:53,017 --> 00:20:58,001
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you are averse to the mind-affected will, because it feels uncomfortable, feels threatening, feels unpleasant.

207
00:20:58,742 --> 00:20:59,502
[SPEAKER_01]: Same with sensuality.

208
00:20:59,522 --> 00:21:00,263
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I'm saying, people.

209
00:21:00,663 --> 00:21:18,979
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you need to start restraining, because basically, for as long as you don't cultivate that restraint sufficiently enough, whether you want it or not, whether you think about it or not, you will believe, you will genuinely exist in the form of belief that sensuality and desire comes from sense objects.

210
00:21:20,425 --> 00:21:26,146
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when you say no to that, the pain that comes as a result of it, you think it came because I said no.

211
00:21:28,546 --> 00:21:36,448
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you actually stop giving in to sensuality, regardless of what you believe, stop fueling it, the time will come when you recognize.

212
00:21:36,468 --> 00:21:49,110
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, if you practice renunciation long enough, you'll see that not just that renunciation doesn't cause the pain of... that you experience when you say no to something, it reveals it.

213
00:21:49,928 --> 00:21:51,169
[SPEAKER_01]: As something that was already there.

214
00:21:52,790 --> 00:21:57,635
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the pain that you experience when you say no to your desires, came from the desires.

215
00:21:58,415 --> 00:21:59,977
[SPEAKER_01]: Saying no just highlighted it.

216
00:22:00,877 --> 00:22:11,567
[SPEAKER_01]: But for somebody with a completely skewed view of where the emphasis is, sense objects, good, bad, just linear, plus, minus, do not allow self-awareness in regard to either.

217
00:22:12,335 --> 00:22:21,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Means you're not allowing the mind to be seen and discerned, which is a prerequisite for sotāpanna, which means everything else that the Buddha taught sotāpanna simply does not apply to you.

218
00:22:23,779 --> 00:22:32,403
[SPEAKER_00]: And so you mentioned the three fetters in relation to now discerning the citta rightly, but how does personality view fit into that?

219
00:22:33,383 --> 00:22:33,663
[SPEAKER_01]: It's same.

220
00:22:34,303 --> 00:22:36,444
[SPEAKER_01]: The extent of the emphasis on the external.

221
00:22:37,705 --> 00:22:38,925
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly what Athavāda is.

222
00:22:42,987 --> 00:22:57,113
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the view that you're kind of... the center of your being, the center of who you are, the center of I am, the center of my point of view, it's external to this experience as a whole.

223
00:22:59,514 --> 00:23:06,717
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, the sensuality is external to these desires, to these cravings, to these actions.

224
00:23:10,689 --> 00:23:18,234
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why by addressing the wrong emphasis that has proliferated into the assumption of it comes from over there, all these things will disappear.

225
00:23:19,055 --> 00:23:23,078
[SPEAKER_01]: These first three factors, because that was the common... that was the basis for all three of them.

